==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*terh₁-}}
15th century, {{lbor|en|la|attritio|t=a rubbing against|nocap=1}}, from the verb {{m|la|attritus}}, past participle of {{m|la|atterere|t=to wear}}, from {{compound|la|ad-|t1=to, towards|terere|t2=to rub|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈtɹɪʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-attrition.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪʃən|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Grinding down or wearing away by [[friction]].
# The [[gradual]] reduction in a tangible or intangible resource due to causes that are passive and do not involve productive use of the resource.
# {{lb|en|human resources}} A gradual, natural reduction in [[membership]] or [[personnel]], as through retirement, resignation, or [[death]].
# {{lb|en|sciences}} The loss of [[participant]]s during an [[experiment]].
# {{lb|en|theology}} Imperfect [[contrition]] or remorse.
# {{lb|en|dentistry}} The wearing of teeth due to their grinding.
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} The loss of a first or second language or a portion of that language.

